    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon.jpg" alt="Napoleon" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jacques-Louis David, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The German Confederation (<em>Deutscher Bund</em>) was created at the Congress of Vienna to fill the space left by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, dissolved in 1806, and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confederation-of-the-rhine">Confederation of the Rhine</a>, which had collapsed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leipzig">Battle of Leipzig</a> in 1813. German patriots had hoped for a national state, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> under Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klemens-von-metternich">Klemens von Metternich</a>, the middle states such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and Württemberg, and the great powers all preferred a league that would preserve the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> of its members and the balance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-federal-act">Federal Act</a> (<em>Bundesakte</em>) of 8 June 1815, incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/final-act-of-the-congress-of-vienna">Final Act of the Congress of Vienna</a>, joined 39 sovereign states and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free cities</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a> for their former imperial lands, kingdoms such as Bavaria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, Württemberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, dozens of grand duchies, duchies and principalities, and the free cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bremen">Bremen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>. Several foreign monarchs were members through German possessions: the King of the United Kingdom as King of Hanover until 1837, the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> for Holstein and Lauenburg, and the King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> for Luxembourg and, from 1839, Limburg. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna-final-act">Vienna Final Act</a> of 1820 completed the constitution of the league.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nuremberg.jpg" alt="Nuremberg" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:N%C3%BCrnberger_Burg_im_Herbst_2013.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DALIBRI, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Confederation covered a society that was still largely rural, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> and seigneurial dues being dismantled at different speeds from state to state, and with a population that grew from roughly 30 million to well over 40 million by the 1860s. Religion divided it between a Catholic south and west and a Protestant north and east, and confessional politics remained important in every member state. The league itself had almost no economic powers, and its members kept their own currencies, tolls and weights. Economic integration came instead through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zollverein">Zollverein</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/customs-union">customs union</a> founded under Prussian leadership on 1 January 1834, which excluded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and gradually drew in most of the other states. Railways spread rapidly after the line from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/furth">Fürth</a> opened in 1835 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> line followed in 1839, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> and Silesia began to industrialise. Rural poverty, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> failures and the hunger crisis of 1846–47, and the plight of the Silesian weavers, whose revolt was crushed in 1844, drove emigration, above all to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The period was also one of extraordinary cultural achievement in philosophy, music, scholarship and the universities, though this owed little to the federal institutions themselves.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dybbol"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-dybbol.jpg" alt="Battle of Dybbøl" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dybbol">Battle of Dybbøl</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:D%C3%BCpplerSchanzen1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lambiotte, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Confederation’s first purpose was collective defence: an attack on any member was to be an attack on all, and members undertook not to make war on each other or ally against the league. Its Federal Army (<em>Bundesheer</em>) consisted of contingents from the member states, organised under the military constitution of 1821 into ten army corps, of which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> each supplied three, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> one and the other states the remaining three, with a commander chosen by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagsatzung">Diet</a> only in time of war. The league maintained federal fortresses at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>, Luxembourg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landau">Landau</a>, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine-crisis">Rhine Crisis of 1840</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> it built new ones at Ulm and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastatt">Rastatt</a>. The federal army never fought a major war as a whole. In 1848–49 contingents were used against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> and against revolutionaries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, and in 1863 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> and Hanoverian troops carried out a federal execution in Holstein. The decisive campaigns of the period were waged by Austria and Prussia themselves: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-schleswig-war">Second Schleswig War</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1864, culminating in the storming of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-dybbol">Dybbøl</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-prussian-war">Austro-Prussian War</a> of 1866, in which the federal contingents of the middle states fought alongside Austria and were defeated, while Prussia won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-koniggratz">Battle of Königgrätz</a> on 3 July 1866.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/carlsbad-decrees"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carlsbad-decrees.jpg" alt="Carlsbad Decrees" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/carlsbad-decrees">Carlsbad Decrees</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:German_Conservatism_Flags_Symbol.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hstoops, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Confederation had no head of state, no executive, no common citizenship and no federal courts. Its only organ was the Federal Assembly or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagsatzung">Diet</a> (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-convention-german-confederation">Bundesversammlung</a></em>), a permanent congress of envoys of the member governments meeting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palais-thurn-und-taxis-frankfurt">Thurn und Taxis Palace</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> am Main under the presidency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Ordinary business went to an Inner Council of 17 votes, in which the largest states had one vote each and the smaller ones shared collective votes, while fundamental questions went to a Plenum in which votes were weighted and some decisions required unanimity. Article 13 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-federal-act">Federal Act</a> promised every state an assembly of estates, which led to constitutions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> in 1818 and Württemberg in 1819, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klemens-von-metternich">Metternich</a> used the Diet to contain liberalism. After the student <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-ludwig-sand">Karl Ludwig Sand</a> murdered the writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/august-von-kotzebue">August von Kotzebue</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlsbad-decrees">Carlsbad Decrees</a> of 1819 imposed censorship, supervised the universities and set up an investigating commission at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hambach-festival">the Hambach Festival</a> of 1832 the Six Articles and further measures tightened control. In 1848 the Diet yielded its authority to the Imperial Regent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archduke-john-of-austria">Archduke John of Austria</a>, appointed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-parliament">Frankfurt Parliament</a>, but it was restored in 1850–51 after the failure of the revolution and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>’s retreat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olmütz</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vienna"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vienna.jpg" alt="Vienna" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schoenbrunn_philharmoniker_2012.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Superbass, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The German Confederation shaped modern German and Central European history in several lasting ways. It kept the peace among the German states for half a century and gave the European order created at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> a stable centre, while preventing the formation of a German national state that the great powers feared. Its repressive policies made it a symbol of reaction to liberals and nationalists, but within its framework constitutional government took root in the southern states, and the national movement grew through gymnastics clubs, choral societies, student fraternities and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg-festival">Wartburg Festival</a> of 1817. The revolution of 1848 produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-parliament">Frankfurt Parliament</a> in St Paul’s Church, whose Constitution of 28 March 1849 and its catalogue of basic rights later influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar">Weimar</a> and post-war West German constitutions; the black, red and gold flag that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagsatzung">Diet</a> itself adopted in 1848 became the German national colours. The Confederation also framed the central question of German unification: a ‘greater German’ solution including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> or a ‘lesser German’ one under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. Its federal structure, with sovereign states represented by government envoys, influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundesrat-german-empire">Bundesrat of the North German Confederation</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>, an element that survives in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bundesrat-germany">Bundesrat of the Federal Republic of Germany</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-koniggratz"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-koniggratz.jpg" alt="Battle of Königgrätz" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-koniggratz">Battle of Königgrätz</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schlacht-bei-koeniggraetz-von-georg-bleibtreu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Georg Bleibtreu, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Confederation depended on cooperation between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, and that cooperation broke down in the 1850s and 1860s. In 1849 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-william-iv-of-prussia">Frederick William IV of Prussia</a> refused the imperial crown offered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-parliament">Frankfurt Parliament</a> but tried to unite northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erfurt-union">Erfurt Union</a>, a plan he abandoned under Austrian and Russian pressure in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punctation-of-olmutz">Punctation of Olmütz</a> in November 1850. Prussia, represented at the restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tagsatzung">Diet</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a> from 1851, then contested Austria’s leadership at every turn, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zollverein">Zollverein</a> bound the smaller states’ economies to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. Austria’s defeat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1859 and the failure of its reform plan at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-congress-of-princes">Frankfurt Congress of Princes</a> in 1863, which Prussia boycotted, weakened it further. After the joint war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1864, the two powers quarrelled over the administration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Schleswig</a> and Holstein. When Austria referred the question to the Diet and the Diet voted on 14 June 1866 to mobilise federal forces against Prussia, Prussia declared the Confederation dissolved. Its victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-koniggratz">Königgrätz</a> was decisive, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-prague-1866">Peace of Prague</a> of 23 August 1866 Austria accepted the end of the Confederation. Prussia annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kassel">Kassel</a>, Nassau and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> and formed the North German Confederation in 1867, the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> proclaimed in 1871.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The German Confederation was the framework of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> and Bismarck: a league of sovereign states that kept the peace and repressed liberalism for half a century, saw the national revolution of 1848, and was destroyed by the Austro-Prussian rivalry that decided how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> would be unified.</em></p>

